# AI Agent Operating Rule

**Applies to every coding agent in this repo** (Claude, Cursor, Codex, and future agents). Canonical copy: `docs/ai-agent-operating-rule.md`.

Optimize **cost and token usage** without sacrificing **engineering quality**. Use the smallest capable model; escalate for risk and hard review. Parallelize only with strict disjoint ownership.

## Capability classes (provider-agnostic)

| Class | Use for |
|-------|---------|
| **Fast** | Lookups, tiny edits, formatting, log summaries, mechanical refactors |
| **Standard** | Default implementation, routine refactors/tests, moderate debugging, single-surface UI |
| **Strong** | Architecture, auth/payments/migrations/security, deep review, hard multi-system debugging, production validation |
| **Frontier** | Broad redesigns, contested multi-agent plans, ambiguous architecture, very expensive-mistake work |

Default to **Standard**. De-escalate mechanical work to **Fast**. Escalate when blast radius or ambiguity rises. If a requested model is missing, use the nearest same-class model and state the substitution once.

**Never** spend Strong/Frontier on work Fast/Standard can finish safely. **Never** leave high-blast-radius work on Fast because it is cheaper.

## Work splitting

Prefer **plan → execute → review** on non-trivial tasks:

1. Plan with Strong/Frontier when needed.
2. Execute narrow slices with Standard/Fast.
3. Review/validate with Strong on high risk; Standard on ordinary work.

If the task is simple enough for **one agent**, that agent may plan, implement, review, test, and **commit** alone. Do not invent multi-agent ceremony for small work.

One objective per agent. Escalating mid-flight is required when ambiguity, security risk, or failing invariants appear.

## Token and context control

1. Read narrowly; prefer search and partial reads.
2. Do not re-read without a reason.
3. Prefer diffs/symbols over dumping unchanged files.
4. Offload exploration; return summaries.
5. Keep plans short and checkable.
6. Do not paste large rule bodies into prompts when this rule already applies.
7. Stop when acceptance criteria are met.
8. Batch independent tool calls.
9. Discard failed hypotheses quickly.

A failed Fast attempt that forces a full Strong redo costs more than starting at Standard.

## Quality bar (non-negotiable)

1. Root cause over patches.
2. Minimal impact — touch only what is required.
3. Prove it works (tests/lint/repro) before claiming done.
4. Staff-engineer review standard.
5. High-risk zones need Strong review even if cheaper models wrote the code: auth/sessions, Stripe/webhooks, migrations, middleware order, cross-tenant boundaries, onboarding/access gating.
6. Testing strategy scales with risk.
7. If quality slips, escalate model class and fix — never ship a cheap wrong answer.

## Parallel agent coordination

Parallelism is allowed for large tasks that benefit. Safety beats speed.

**Hard rules when multiple writers are active:**

1. **Strict disjoint file ownership** — each writable file has at most one active owner.
2. **No shared writable files** — integration files stay with the **lead agent**.
3. **No overlapping edit areas** — collide ⇒ serialize or re-scope.
4. **No git commits from parallel worker agents** — workers do not add/commit/push/PR.
5. **Lead agent integrates** — merge order, verification, task docs, and all commits.
6. **Workers report within their lane** — no freelancing into other files.
7. Read-only lanes may share reads; write lanes must not share writes.
8. Ownership collision ⇒ **stop and re-plan**.

**Single-agent exception:** one agent handling a simple task may implement, review, test, and commit its own changes.

## Quick decision guide

| Situation | Action |
|-----------|--------|
| Tiny localized tweak | Single Fast/Standard agent; commit own change |
| Clear single-feature slice | Single Standard agent; self-review + tests |
| Auth/payment/migration/security | Implement carefully; Strong review required |
| Large redesign, independent files | Lead plans; parallel disjoint workers; lead commits |
| Ambiguous architecture | Strong/Frontier plan first; do not parallelize into fog |
| Worker needs out-of-lane file | Stop; return to lead; reassign ownership |
